    <statement><p>The formula 
<m>\displaystyle \lim_{h\to 0} \frac{\left(3+h\right)^{3}-27}{h}</m></p>
<p>represents <m>f^\prime</m> <m>(a)</m> for some function <m>f</m> and some number <m>a</m>. 
Find <m>f(x)</m> and <m>a</m>.</p>
<p><m>f(x) =</m> <fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
<p><m>a=</m> <fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0002"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>x^{3}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>3</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>f(x)=x^{3}</m>. Then <m>f(x+h)=\left(x+h\right)^{3}</m> so with <m>a=3</m>, then <m>f(a+h)=\left(3+h\right)^{3}</m>
and <m>f(a)=27</m>.



Hence, 
<m>\displaystyle f'(3)=  \lim_{h\to 0} \frac{\left(3+h\right)^{3}-27}{h}</m>.


</solution>
